From 4a4862068409b13ed08f73fdf16454d06f66549a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ender Hsieh <andhsieh@nvidia.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 02:45:13 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] nvidia-gpu: react to MCTP endpoint connectivity

An MCTP endpoint can stop answering while the device behind it stays
configured. Nothing notices today: the device keeps polling an endpoint
that is not there, so its sensors keep reporting the last value they
happened to read rather than saying they have nothing to report.

Polling now starts when the endpoint is confirmed online rather than
when the sensors are built, so the SMA device tests say so explicitly
instead of expecting init() alone to put a request on the wire.

Track each endpoint through a small state machine and drive it from
mctpd's Connectivity property:

  Init       --(device init() done)-------->  Online
  Online     --(Connectivity "Degraded")--->  Recovering
  Recovering --(Connectivity "Available")-->  Online

Degraded stops the poll and tells the sensors they are not functional,
Available resumes it. How an unavailable reading is represented is the
sensor's business, so the device says only that it cannot read. The
D-Bus objects are kept across the transitions, so a consumer sees the
same objects go unavailable rather than objects that come and go.

Only the SMA is wired to this for now. mctpd's .Recover probes an
endpoint by physical address, and a device behind the SMA bridge has
none of its own, its phys is the bridge's, so the probe only reaches
the SMA. Rediscovery of bridged devices is a follow-up change.

Tested: Build an image for nvl32-obmc machine with the following
patches cherry-picked:

```
1. Align with upstream u-boot dts tree:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/89932
2. mctpd configuration:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/87390
3. Enable nvidia-gpu sensor:
   https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/89933

Deployed nvidiagpusensor and drove a recovery cycle on the SMA bridge
endpoint via mctpd .Recover:
- Connectivity "Degraded": the endpoint went offline; polling stopped,
  Functional=false, and the temperature sensor read NaN.
- Connectivity "Available": the endpoint returned online; polling
  resumed and Functional returned to true.
- D-Bus objects preserved throughout; service active with no restarts.
```
1. Verified startup poll, Degraded -> offline, Available -> online via
   the nvidiagpusensor journal and busctl on the BMC.
2. EndpointState transition unit tests pass.

[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/91169/27

